From what I read in the source code (but I could be missing some
other relevant parts), the probability is 1 in 10 +
2*(gift_count * artifact_count), where gift_count is the number
of artifacts received as a gift from your god, and
artifact_count is the total number already created in the game.
So if no artifacts exist yet, it's only 1-in-10, and if the only
artifact is the one he's already been given, it's 1-in-12.
Significantly better than 1-in-25.

It's an interesting formula---creating an artifact by naming
something or by wishing doesn't affect the probability of the
first gift, but it has very definite negative consequences from
then on out.  It also means that any randomly created artifacts
(that he may not have seen yet) will have a negative effect,
once he's received the first gift.  (It also means that a
guaranteed first gift can be seen as a significant disadvantage,
unless it is for something that you really want, like Magicbane,
since the probability of further artifacts decreases with the
square, and not linearly.)

Of course, we all know the RGN.  I've had games where I've
gotten Magicbane on my first sacrifice, and Frostbane on my
third.  Just as I've had games where I've never gotten an
artifact gift.  (The only healer I ascended only saw the
guaranteed artifacts in the entire game.  Dispite a fair bit of
sacrificing.)

